  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The model empirical evaluation relies exclusively on data collected from a simulated driving experiment rather than real-world travel choices.",
      "what": "The authors developed a Latent Class Reinforcement Learning (LCRL) model estimated via Variational Bayes to model how dynamic preferences evolve alongside individual heterogeneity. Applying the model to a driving simulator dataset revealed three distinct behavioral subgroups: one with context-dependent preferences and context-specific exploitation, one adhering to a persistent exploitative strategy across all contexts, and one combining an exploratory strategy with context-specific preferences.",
      "why": "The framework provides a method to model individual differences in how travelers learn and update their choices over time, replacing static preference assumptions with adaptive, feedback-driven decision rules."
    }
